    <item>
      <title>Millions of Americans are not taking their GLP-1 drugs as prescribed</title>
      <author>
                    Beth Mole
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/11/millions-in-us-use-unapproved-glp-1s-and-use-them-in-unapproved-ways/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/health/2026/11/millions-in-us-use-unapproved-glp-1s-and-use-them-in-unapproved-ways/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">93ee3a4441dddbc60c5a5c6d98fcfe69bf0ec377</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:59:01 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Cost and access barriers are pushing people to modified use and unapproved versions.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/GettyImages-2291507729-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/GettyImages-2291507729-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/GettyImages-2291507729-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/GettyImages-2291507729-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/GettyImages-2291507729-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;        Woman using an obesity drug injection pen.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/woman-using-semaglutide-injection-pen-royalty-free-image/2291507729?phrase=obesity%20drug&amp;amp;searchscope=image,film&amp;amp;adppopup=true"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Getty | Munro                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;GLP-1 medications are everywhere these days. The peptide-based drugs are both highly effective and wildly popular for diabetes, weight loss and a growing range of connected chronic health conditions, including cardiovascular disease and obstructive sleep apnea. But, amid the boom, the latest data suggests millions of Americans have turned to unapproved versions and are using the potent drugs in ways doctors never intended, raising questions about what that real-world use means for safety and efficacy.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/75/wr/mm7544a1.htm?s_cid=OS_mm7544a1_e&amp;amp;ACSTrackingID=USCDC_921-DM160420&amp;amp;ACSTrackingLabel=Week%20in%20MMWR%3A%20Vol.%2075%2C%20November%2012%2C%202026&amp;amp;deliveryName=USCDC_921-DM160420"&gt;a study published this week&lt;/a&gt;, researchers at the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention broke down survey data of GLP-1 use collected between July and August of 2025. Based on responses from over 8,000 US adults, the researchers estimate that about one in 10 US adults (9.8 percent) used a glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist, aka GLP-1 medication, in 2025. That’s around 27 million adults. The study was published in the CDC’s Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Among GLP-1 users, nearly half (44 percent) reported using their GLP-1 in a way that was different from how it was prescribed. Users skipped doses, delayed getting refills, and used smaller doses than prescribed, among other changes. More than half of the people modifying how they used the drug said the changes were because of cost or availability reasons. Cost and access barriers were most pronounced among younger GLP-1 users. Fifty-eight percent of adults 14­–39 years old who modified their use cited cost as a barrier, compared with just 20 percent of adults 65 or older who said the same.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Modified use of GLP-1 medications was particularly common among those using compounded versions of GLP-1 medications. Among all GLP-1 users, at least 23 percent used a compounded version; an additional 9 percent said they didn’t know whether their GLP-1 was compounded or not.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;h2&gt;Potential risks&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;These versions of GLP-1 drugs are made by compounding pharmacies and are intended to mimic a medication approved by the Food and Drug Administration, such as Wegovy or Ozempic. But compounded drugs themselves are not approved by the FDA; the regulatory agency does not review their safety, effectiveness, or quality before they’re sold. While compounded drugs are considered more accessible and affordable, they generally have higher rates of preparation errors, contamination, and other manufacturing problems compared to non-compounded drugs.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Among people who used these unapproved, potentially riskier GLP-1s, 62 percent were not taking them as prescribed. That’s compared to just 36 percent of GLP-1 users who took brand-name drugs.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The potential risks of taking unapproved GLP-1 drugs at all or taking them in unapproved ways may be missed by users, the CDC researchers note. &lt;a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama-health-forum/fullarticle/2829225"&gt;A 2025 study&lt;/a&gt; assessing websites selling compounded GLP-1 medications found that nearly 40 percent of sites stated or implied the drugs were FDA-approved, making it harder for consumers to differentiate the compounded versions from FDA-approved ones. Further, nearly 80 percent of sites used brand names in their advertising, which could further mislead consumers. The researchers noted that using brand names violated FDA regulatory standards.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Given the substantial use of compounded GLP-1 medications, understanding the safety and quality of compounded GLP-1 medications is an important public health consideration,” the authors conclude. They also call for more research on the common modifications people are using, such as dose and frequency adjustments, saying the data on the safety and effectiveness of these real-world practices “could help guide the development and implementation of clinical guidelines and support appropriate medication use.”&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>cost</category>
      <category>glp-1</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>FAA expands AI advice to manage air traffic congestion across entire US airspace</title>
      <author>
                    Jeremy Hsu
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/features/2026/11/faa-expands-ai-advice-to-manage-air-traffic-congestion-across-entire-us-airspace/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/features/2026/11/faa-expands-ai-advice-to-manage-air-traffic-congestion-across-entire-us-airspace/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">54aeb16be25773f51a3a68db3d2f2197ec485a98</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:20:47 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         Airlines praised the AI tool, but the air traffic controllers’ union wasn’t involved.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Pilots-during-DFW-departure-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="Two pilots at work during departure of Dallas Fort Worth Airport in United States of America." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Pilots-during-DFW-departure-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Pilots-during-DFW-departure-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Pilots-during-DFW-departure-768x432.jpg 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Pilots-during-DFW-departure-1536x864.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       Two pilots at work during departure of Dallas-Fort Worth Airport in United States of America.                &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/photo/flightdeck-view-royalty-free-image/666297356"               target="_blank"&gt;                      Rathke | Getty Images                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;The US government has expanded the use of an AI tool for advising air traffic controllers to assist in managing air traffic across the entire national airspace system. Airline executives have voiced support for the rollout, but the US labor union representing air traffic controllers has said it was not involved in designing or testing the AI-driven system.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) says the $875 million SMART system has helped reroute 180 flights since &lt;a href="https://www.faa.gov/newsroom/trumps-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy-delivers-state-art-air-traffic-control"&gt;being implemented&lt;/a&gt; in airspace over Washington, DC, on September 21. The agency claims that this saved an average of 10 minutes per flight and almost 80 gallons of fuel per flight—and it is apparently hoping such results can be replicated nationwide through the expanded deployment.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“After a successful initial rollout to the National Capital Region in September, the FAA will now use SMART in the airspace of all 22 centers across the country to reduce delays, reduce fuel consumption and improve the travel experience for the flying public,” according to the agency &lt;a href="https://www.faa.gov/newsroom/statements/general-statements"&gt;statement posted&lt;/a&gt; on November 10.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/smart-fact-sheet"&gt;FAA fact sheet&lt;/a&gt; describes SMART as a cloud-based system that combines information about “weather patterns, flight paths, traffic flow and controller staffing metrics” to predict air traffic patterns. It is designed to make scheduling recommendations, identify optimal flight routes, and determine the best order of arrivals for aircraft.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;However, a human team of FAA specialists based in DC is ultimately responsible for validating the SMART system recommendations before passing them along to air traffic control facilities across the country, according to the agency.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;Furthermore, the AI-powered SMART system “cannot be used to replace the critical role air traffic controllers play or take over control of an aircraft,” the FAA clarified in the September 21 announcement about the initial deployment. Local air traffic controllers can decide whether or not to accept the SMART system recommendations.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The previous FAA announcement included supportive statements from Delta Air Lines CEO Ed Bastian and American Airlines CEO Robert Isom.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;But the National Air Traffic Controllers Association, which represents more than 15,000 members, also &lt;a href="https://www.flyingmag.com/faa-smart-ai-rollout-natca-atc-union/"&gt;told Flying magazine&lt;/a&gt; that it was not consulted or involved in the “design, testing, or implementation of SMART and therefore cannot speak to its effect on the air traffic control workforce.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Following the initial deployment in airspace above DC, the FAA expanded the SMART system’s coverage to air route traffic control centers in Atlanta along with Jacksonville and Miami in Florida, according to &lt;a href="https://www.dallasnews.com/business/airlines/article/faa-ai-air-traffic-tool-reduces-dfw-flight-delays-22520053.php"&gt;The Dallas Morning News&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The FAA also shared an example with The Dallas Morning News about how SMART recommended a shorter and faster route for a cargo flight arriving at Dallas-Fort Worth International Airport, although the newspaper noted that the agency did not identify the flight involved and has not provided information about how SMART may have assisted passenger flight traffic.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Ars has previously covered the &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/09/faa-tees-up-875m-ai-tool-to-help-manage-air-traffic-congestion/"&gt;SMART system’s development&lt;/a&gt; and lingering questions about how it works, as well as the air traffic controller shortage and other challenges facing the FAA.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
